An anonymous struct reaches every backend as an ordinary named struct.

The checker interns one synthesized `*ir.StructDef` per canonical field
signature and registers it in `pkg.Structs`, so by the time a generator runs
there is no such thing as an anonymous struct -- only a declaration whose name
the program did not write. That is the whole point of the design: Go emits a
`type` and a composite literal, Kotlin a data class and a constructor call, JS
a factory function, and none of the three carries a case for the construct.

The name is `__anon_` plus the field names plus six hex digits of the canonical
signature. It has to be one identifier in Go, Kotlin and JS at once, which
leaves nothing but letters, digits and underscore to build it from; the hash is
what keeps two structs whose fields are named alike but typed differently
apart, and `claimTopLevel` refuses the prefix to a program so the synthesized
name cannot collide with a declared one.

`pair` and `swapped` differ only in the order their fields are written, and
`sumPair`'s parameter is the type written out. All four are one declaration in
the output -- if they were not, the golden would carry two or three near-identical
structs and the assignment between them would not compile.

`empty` is the field-less signature, and it is here because `sngl:builtin`
interns that one first, for `effect<T = struct {}>`. While the intern cache was
keyed by signature alone the program got back builtin's declaration and its own
package never received one, so every target emitted five uses of a type it had
not declared.

It is keyed on an `effect` for that reason and for one more: `emptyWidth(e) => 0`
ignores its argument and folds, so a program whose only read of `empty` is that
call leaves the var dead and the shake takes the declaration with it. The whole
paragraph above then pins nothing, while the archive still reads as though it
does -- and the Kotlin defect below reads as fixed. An effect's key is read on
every settle and cannot be folded away.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `(?s)type __anon_a_b_\w+ struct.*type __anon_a_b_` -- four spellings of one signature are one declaration, not two that happen to look alike
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `struct\{\}\{` -- a literal names its declared type; `struct{}{A: 1, B: 2}` is what a decl-less anonymous struct emitted, and it does not compile
broken out/kotlin/android -- an empty anonymous struct emits a Kotlin `data class` with no primary constructor parameter, which the language forbids
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

func sumPair(p struct {
    a int
    b int
}) => p.a + p.b

func emptyWidth(e struct {}) => 0

const a = 1
const b = 2

component main node {
    var (
        empty = {}
        pair = {a=a, b=b}
        swapped = {b=4, a=3}
        shorthand = {a, b}
        written = struct {
            a int
            b int
        }{a=5, b=6}
        total = sumPair(pair) + sumPair(swapped) + sumPair(shorthand) + sumPair(written)
        mounts = 0
    )

    effect(on=empty, @mount(v) { mounts = mounts + 1 })

    text(value=string(total))
    text(value=string(pair.a))
    text(value=string(sumPair(pair) + emptyWidth(empty)))
    text(value=string(mounts))
}

window {
    main
}
